### Differentiating using the power rule

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(x^{-1}\right)
\\\\
&=-1\cdot x^{-1-1}\qquad\gray{\text{The power rule}}
\\\\
&=-x^{-2}
\end{align}$

### Evaluating the derivative

So we found that $\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\dfrac{1}{x}\right)=-x^{-2}$, which can also be written as $-\dfrac{1}{x^2}$.

Now let's plug $\blueD{x=6}$:

$\begin{align}
-\dfrac{1}{(\blueD{6})^2}&=-\dfrac{1}{36}
\end{align}$
